<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Turkistan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yasi, Hazrat-e Turkestan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Turkistan is a city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>, known in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> as Yasi. It grew around the shrine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-yasawi">Ahmad Yasawi</a>, whose mausoleum was built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>. From the late 16th century it was a political and spiritual centre of the Kazakh Khanate, where many khans were buried.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Turkistan is the administrative centre of Turkistan Region in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> river, 160 kilometres north-west of Shymkent on the Trans-Aral Railway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyzylorda">Kyzylorda</a> to the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> to the south. Its name comes from the Turco-Persian phrase Hazrat-i-Turkistan, 'Saint of Turkistan', a reference to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-yasawi">Ahmad Yasawi</a>, a prominent 11th-century poet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> who rests in the city. Earlier, and through much of the medieval and early modern era, it was called Iasy or Shavgar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city has an archaeological record reaching back to the 4th century. It emerged as a commercial centre after the decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otrar">Otrar</a>, an ancient city near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>, and in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-yasawi">Ahmad Yasawi</a> it became a hub of spiritual growth and Islamic education for the people of the Kazakh steppes. In the 1390s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> built a domed mausoleum over Yasawi's resting place, one of the most notable architectural landmarks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>. The city also has a medieval bath-house and four mausoleums, one for Rabiya Sultan Begim, Timur's great-granddaughter, and three for Kazakh khans.</p><p>Before the Russian arrival in the 19th century Turkistan stood on the boundary between the Perso-Islamic oasis civilisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> to the south and the Kazakh steppes to the north. From the 16th to the 18th century it was the capital of the Kazakh Khanate and the political centre of the steppe. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> expanded and weakened the khanate, smaller southern states fell, and by 1864 the Russian general Veryovkin had captured Turkistan from the Kokand Khanate. It became part of the Syr-Darya Oblast in Russian Turkistan, briefly of the Turkistan Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic after 1917–18, and in 1924 of the Kazakh ASSR.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Mausoleum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-yasawi">Khoja Ahmed Yasawi</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and its image appeared on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>'s banknotes until 2006. According to local tradition, visiting Turkistan three times equals a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, and the city has been called the Second Mecca of the East; it draws thousands of pilgrims. In 2018 it became the administrative centre of the renamed Turkistan Region, and the 2019 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 165,000 inhabitants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> making up almost all of them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Turkistan (city)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some farming was practised in the river valleys, and the oasis towns of the middle Syr Darya, among them Sauran, Sygnak, Sayram and <b>Turkistan</b>, gave the khans access to crafts, markets and Muslim scholarship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its tribes, such as the Dulat, Uysun and Jalayir, were nomadic herders closely tied to the oasis cities of Tashkent and <b>Turkistan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/senior-juz">Senior Juz</a></li></ul></section>
